<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m>. Give the position vector, in terms of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, of a point R that lies on the line AB beyond B.</p><div><m>\overrightarrow{OR} =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="ans1"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A point with position vector <m>\lambda\mathbf{a} + \mu\mathbf{b}</m> lies on the line AB exactly when <m>\lambda + \mu = 1</m>. Then <m>\overrightarrow{AR} = \mu\,\overrightarrow{AB}</m>: the point is between A and B when <m>0 \lt \mu \lt 1</m>, beyond B when <m>\mu \gt 1</m> and beyond A when <m>\mu \lt 0</m>. The size of <m>\mu</m> says how many times as long as AB the line AR is.</p><p>For a point beyond B the coefficient of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> must be greater than 1. One answer is <m>{2 \mathbf{b}-\mathbf{a}}</m>; another is <m>{3 \mathbf{b}-2 \mathbf{a}}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>2 \mathbf{b}-\mathbf{a}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>